  <h2 class="h2">Questions about recall &amp; scheduling</h2>
  <div class="faq" style="text-align:left">
    <details class="qa"><summary>What is active recall?</summary><div class="a">Instead of re-reading notes, you try to answer a question from memory. The effort of retrieving the answer is what builds strong, lasting memory — and it's how MedBank's recall decks work.</div></details>
    <details class="qa"><summary>How does the scheduling work?</summary><div class="a">Each card climbs a ladder as you get it right — back after 1 day, then 3 days, then a week, then two weeks, then long-term. A miss drops it to the start, so you see struggling cards sooner and solid ones less often.</div></details>
    <details class="qa"><summary>Do I have to decide what to study?</summary><div class="a">No. Open Active Recall and MedBank has already gathered the cards that are due, overdue or giving you trouble across all your lectures.</div></details>
    <details class="qa"><summary>Can I focus on just my weak cards?</summary><div class="a">Yes — filter a session to only the cards you keep missing, your hard/starred cards, or this week's mistakes.</div></details>
    <details class="qa"><summary>Do I need to study every day?</summary><div class="a">Little and often works best, and a streak helps keep the habit — but the schedule adapts to when you actually review, so you won't lose your progress if you skip a day.</div></details>
